{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794137248,"nonce":"wXtF7l4LW/k5npMH/H9OwAmoWWk=","title":"Plan: a lean GPU stack for Blinc, narrow interface first","message":"Goal: lighter to download, faster to compile, lower memory, and a path to embedded targets. Detail lives in the child issues; this one holds the reasoning and the order.\n\nWhy: the wgpu stack (wgpu, wgpu-core, wgpu-hal, naga, gpu-alloc and their helpers) is 42 crates in the default macOS tree, and six of them (naga, wgpu-core, ash, wgpu-hal, wgpu, metal) took about 40 s of the 209 s of CPU in a clean debug build. Blinc uses a small, specific slice: 208 wgpu items, 39 render and 5 compute pipelines, about 16k lines of WGSL. See the dependency diet issue, 346dacb, for the measurements.\n\nConstraints that shape the plan:\n- Five targets, not three: Metal, Vulkan, D3D12, WebGPU and WebGL2. Browsers accept only WGSL for WebGPU, so a shader language of our own cannot remove WGSL; it would need five emitters. The expensive parts are porting the shaders and the driver quirks wgpu absorbs (we pin wgpu-hal 26.0.5 for a Mesa acquire regression, and the low-end paths already branch on missing storage buffers and independent blend).\n- Precedent: Zed removed its custom Linux renderer for wgpu in February 2026 (PR 46758) and kept native Metal on macOS. Native where it is cheap, wgpu elsewhere, is the pattern that held.\n- MCU-class embedded targets have no GPU API at all; they need a software backend, not a leaner GPU one.\n\nReference: sebbbi/NoGraphicsAPI (C++20, MIT; the library for Sebastian Aaltonen's No Graphics API post and talk). Native Metal 4 and Vulkan 1.4 with VK_EXT_descriptor_heap and VK_EXT_mesh_shader, Slang shaders. Ideas: GPU memory addressed by 64-bit pointers instead of buffer objects, vertex shaders that fetch their own vertices, bindless textures indexed by heap slot, one root pointer per draw instead of descriptor sets and pipeline layouts, barriers described by producer and consumer. It is a low-level library: allocation policy, lifetimes and synchronisation stay with the application. It needs very recent hardware and drivers (its compatibility table is dated 5 September 2026; missing extensions are the main reason a GPU is unsupported) and has no D3D12, WebGPU, WebGL2 or GLES backend, so it fits as a model for a modern-device path, not as a replacement for all of ours. Its Slang choice is relevant to the shader issue.\n\nOrder:\n1. c546365 narrow render backend interface, wgpu behind it, root-arguments question settled.\n2. 709d29f offline shader pipeline (naga tools or Slang), no runtime compiler.\n3. 310506c native Metal backend for macOS and iOS.\n4. 17bce68 CPU raster backend behind DrawContext, and 0560936 no_std audit for the core crates, in parallel with the above; the software backend depends on the audit.\n5. Native Vulkan or D3D12 backends only if a measured need appears. A shader language of our own only if neither naga tools nor Slang fit.\n\nQuick wins that do not wait for any of this (per-target wgpu features, optional SVG, png instead of image, unused dependencies) are tracked in 346dacb.","files":null}]}